From 9b32770ddb0fef2b907d7808cc87e2c8802b541d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 17:54:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: a fidelity step-down level, forced by
 advancedsettings.xml for measurements

Where the GPU cannot draw a native Dolby Vision frame in the time of a
refresh (an integrated GPU with a profile 7 FEL stream), the
reconstruction can trade some of its precision for GPU time. This adds
the mechanism, not the policy: the faithful picture is never changed on
graphics that keep up, and when Kodi is to step down by itself is to be
decided with measurements on real GPUs, which this makes possible.

The renderer of the bridge takes a step-down level
(dvbridge_render_set_step()). The planes that libplacebo scales up to the
size of the picture are scaled up bilinearly, the sampling of the GPU
itself, instead of with Lanczos, more of them at each level: 1, the
chroma of the base layer; 2, also the chroma of the enhancement layer;
3, also its luma. Bilinear and not libplacebo's bicubic, which was
measured further from Lanczos. The planes of the enhancement layer get
upscalers of their own (libplacebo-9920 of the LibreELEC tree, which
this needs): without them they would take the one of the chroma. Level
0 is the default and changes nothing in the parameters of a render.

Measured with the bridge on a software OpenGL ES (zink on lavapipe),
pictures 3, 27 and 51 of the FEL test stream, both ways of drawing and
the picture for output as HDR10: at level 0, and with no level set,
every output is byte for byte the one of the bridge before this change
with the libplacebo before libplacebo-9920 (the reconstructed pictures,
and all 137 lines of hashes and checks that the test prints). Levels 1,
2 and 3 change the reconstructed picture exactly as a test-only
libplacebo that chose the filter of each kind of plane did, in every
pixel: on picture 51, 21.75 %, 48.30 % and 55.61 % of the pixels, most
of them by one code of 4095, none by more than 7, 10 and 16 codes; on
pictures 3 and 27, at most 2.64 % and 13.88 %. How much GPU time each
level saves is not known: that takes a real GPU.

advancedsettings.xml can force a level for every native frame:
<video><dvstepdown>N</dvstepdown></video>, N from 0 to 3. Absent, the
level is Kodi's choice, which is 0 for now. It is read for each native
output and told once in the log. The level of each frame is kept with
its GPU time and written in the lines of the log that give GPU times
(the statistics of every 10 s, the comparison of the two ways of
drawing, the call for presenting frames a frame ahead), so that a run
at each level on a GPU tells the milliseconds each step saves. What is
found of the ways of drawing is kept apart for a forced level, as it is
when the bars are not written first. The output as HDR10 stays at
level 0.
